new file mode 100644
index 0000000..436254a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,58 @@
+# Deploy Fizzy on one server
+
+The root Kamal configuration deploys the open-source edition of Fizzy to one Linux server. It uses SQLite and local Active Storage, runs Solid Queue in the web container, and lets Kamal Proxy provision HTTPS certificates.
+
+## Before you start
+
+You will need:
+
+- A fork of this repository and a GitHub account that can publish a package to GitHub Container Registry (GHCR).
+- A Linux server reachable over SSH. The SSH user must be able to install and run Docker; `kamal setup` bootstraps Docker on supported servers.
+- A DNS name for Fizzy with an A/AAAA record pointing at the server. Ports 80 and 443 must be open from the internet for Let's Encrypt, and the SSH port must be reachable from your deployment machine.
+- An SMTP account if you want Fizzy to send sign-in links and notifications by email.
+
+Install the Ruby/Bundler dependencies from the [development guide](development.md) and make sure Docker is available on the machine where you run Kamal (Kamal builds the image locally by default).
+
+## Configure the deployment
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml` in your fork:
+
+1. Set `image` to `<your-github-username>/fizzy` and `registry.username` to the same GitHub username. Keep `registry.server: ghcr.io` as-is. Use the owner and lowercase repository name of the GHCR package you will publish.
+2. Replace `your-server.example.com` under `servers.web` with the server's SSH hostname or IP address.
+3. Replace `fizzy.example.com` in both `proxy.host` and `env.clear.APP_HOST` with the public DNS name. The DNS record must already resolve to the server before setup so Kamal Proxy can obtain a certificate.
+4. Set `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_DOMAIN`, and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` to values accepted by your mail provider. `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD` are supplied as secrets below. The default configuration uses authenticated SMTP with STARTTLS on port 587. For implicit TLS (typically port 465), set `SMTP_TLS` to `true`; change the port and authentication settings if your provider requires something different.
+
+Create a local secrets file and fill in its values:
+
+```sh
+cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+openssl rand -hex 64 # Put this value in SECRET_KEY_BASE and keep it stable between deploys.
+```
+
+Set `KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD` to a GitHub personal access token with `read:packages` and `write:packages` permissions for the account that owns the GHCR package. Add your SMTP username and password. The `.kamal/secrets` file is ignored by git and excluded from Docker builds; do not commit it.
+
+For browser push notifications, generate a VAPID key pair and put its private/public keys in `.kamal/secrets`:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails runner 'key = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{key.private_key}"; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{key.public_key}"'
+```
+
+Push is optional; leave both VAPID values empty if you do not use it. See the [development guide](development.md) for details.
+
+## Deploy
+
+After confirming the DNS and firewall settings, run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Kamal installs Docker on the server if needed, builds and pushes the image to GHCR, deploys Fizzy, and starts Kamal Proxy with automatic HTTPS. Subsequent releases use:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+The `fizzy-storage` Docker volume is mounted at `/rails/storage`. It holds the SQLite application, queue, cache, and cable databases as well as uploaded files, so these survive container replacement and later deploys. Back up this volume separately: a Docker volume protects data from deploys, but not from server or disk loss.
+
+Solid Queue runs in the same Puma container (`SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA`); no separate worker server or Redis instance is needed. The sample sets one Puma worker and one queue process to suit a small single-server installation. The default builder targets `amd64`; set `builder.arch` to `arm64` for an ARM server. Change `WEB_CONCURRENCY` and `JOB_CONCURRENCY` in `config/deploy.yml` if the server has more capacity.
